{"verdict": "CORRECT", "quotes": [
{"url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grabbist_Hillfort", "quote": "Grabbist Hillfort is an Iron Age oval hillfort or defended enclosure , west of Dunster in Somerset , England. The site is 885.8 feet (270.0 m) long", "claim": "Iron Age oval hillfort in Somerset, 270 metres long"},
{"url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grabbist_Hillfort", "quote": "It is also surrounded by a ditch, which ranges up to 32.8 feet (10.0 m) wide and 7.2 feet (2.2 m) deep.", "claim": "with a ditch up to 10 metres wide and 2.2 metres deep"},
{"url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grabbist_Hillfort", "quote": "on the northern and western sides, there is a second bank", "claim": "A second defensive bank lines the northern and western perimeter."},
{"url": "https://www.digitaldigging.net/grabbist-hillfort-grabbist-hill-dunster-somerset/", "quote": "The enclosure is ovoid in plan and measures 270x67m.", "claim": "oval hillfort in Somerset, 270 metres long"},
{"url": "https://www.digitaldigging.net/grabbist-hillfort-grabbist-hill-dunster-somerset/", "quote": "The ditch here is 2.2m deep and the overall width of the defences is 10m.", "claim": "with a ditch up to 10 metres wide and 2.2 metres deep"},
{"url": "https://www.exmoorher.co.uk/monument/MSO9408", "quote": "A possible Iron Age defended enclosure, defined to the north and west by a double bank and ditch.", "claim": "Iron Age oval hillfort"},
{"url": "https://www.exmoorher.co.uk/monument/MSO9408", "quote": "On the north and west sides are a double bank and ditch", "claim": "A second defensive bank lines the northern and western perimeter."}
], "right_value": null, "severity": null, "reasoning": "Wikipedia (scheduled monument 1021060, west of Dunster) gives an Iron Age oval hillfort 270 m long with a ditch up to 10 m wide and 2.2 m deep and a second bank on the north and west. The RCHME survey text reproduced on Digital Digging gives an ovoid enclosure of 270x67m and a ditch 2.2m deep; the Exmoor HER independently records the double bank and ditch on the north and west. Caveats: the HER copies of the same RCHME text read 500x60m (an older record c.200m x 100m), and the independent sources give 10 m as the overall width of the defences rather than of the ditch alone; neither is a clear contradiction of the served text."}

